# Line-ending policy, owned by the REPOSITORY rather than by each machine's
# Git installation.
#
# Why this file exists at all. When it was written, core.autocrlf was set in
# neither the local nor the global config — it was `true` in the SYSTEM config,
# which is where the Git for Windows installer puts the answer you gave it
# during setup. That makes the policy a property of the INSTALL, not of the
# project: a second Windows machine whose Git was installed with
# "checkout as-is, commit as-is" gets `false`, commits CRLF into the index, and
# every file in the tree then reads as modified on the first machine. Nothing
# about the content changed. This repo is edited on two Windows machines (the
# local checkout and the VDI), so that is not a hypothetical.
#
# Measured before adding this file: all 105 tracked files were already `i/lf`
# in the index (`git ls-files --eol`). So `text=auto` below matches what is
# already stored and renormalizes NOTHING. That is why it was added at this
# moment — the same line costs a whole-tree diff once a single CRLF file has
# landed.

# Default for everything: normalize to LF in the repository, check out in the
# platform's native ending. Git decides text-vs-binary by content, so a file
# type not listed below is still handled sanely.
* text=auto


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scripts whose line endings are not a preference
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# cmd.exe can mis-parse a .bat/.cmd saved with LF — labels and `goto` are the
# usual casualties, and the failure is a silently skipped line rather than an
# error. None are tracked today; the rule is here so the first one added is
# right without anyone remembering this.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# PowerShell 5.1 (what the VDI runs) does execute LF scripts, so this is
# convention and caution rather than a fix for a known break: these two files
# are Windows-only by nature and anyone opening them in a basic Windows editor
# should not see one long line.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# The opposite case: a shell script with CRLF fails on the very first line,
# because the `\r` becomes part of the interpreter path in the shebang.
# Relevant to the Docker images, which are Linux.
*.sh text eol=lf
mvnw  text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated frontend bundle
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `npm run build` writes here (vite.config.ts outDir) and the output IS
# committed on purpose — frontend/.gitignore explains why: the VDI is expected
# to run the jar without building the UI first.
#
# -diff:  the bundle and its 1.2 MB sourcemap are machine-written. A textual
#         diff of them is noise that buries the real changes in the same commit.
# -merge: two machines that both ran a build produce different hashed files and
#         there is no sensible way to merge them. Git now refuses to try and
#         marks a conflict instead. THE RESOLUTION IS ALWAYS TO REBUILD, never
#         to hand-edit or to pick a side — a half-merged bundle would still
#         load, and would be wrong in a way no test here would catch.
uptest-orchestrator/src/main/resources/static/ui/** -diff -merge

# Same reasoning, less severe: npm writes it, nobody reads the diff. Merge is
# left enabled because the fix for a conflicted lockfile is a plain
# `npm install`, which regenerates it.
package-lock.json -diff


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary types
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# None are tracked today. Listed anyway because the cost of getting it wrong is
# asymmetric: `text=auto` guesses from content, and a binary it guesses wrong
# about is CORRUPTED by newline translation, not merely shown badly.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.eot  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.jar  binary
*.zip  binary
*.xlsx binary
*.mv.db binary

# SVG is left OUT of that list on purpose: it is XML, it is hand-edited here,
# and a readable diff is worth more than treating it as an opaque blob.
